the queen and other world leaders are  paying tribute to archbishop desmond  tutu the anti-apartheid leader who  inspired millions to challenge a racist  government died sunday he was 90 years  old in a statement the queen said tutu  tirelessly championed human rights in  south africa and across the world former  president barack obama hailed him as a  moral compass and president biden and  the first lady spoke of the archbishop's  courage and warmth deborah potter looks  back at tutu's extraordinary life  the tiny clergy man with a twinkle in  his eye  a massive laugh and a dance in his step  born in the township of clarksdale south  africa desmond tutu went on to become a  nobel peace prize winner  a global campaigner for human rights and  a man who always spoke truth to power  whether it was a white racist regime or  corrupt african dictatorship  as south africa's first black archbishop  he unhesitatingly used his office to  bravely challenge the brutal racism of  the country's apartheid rulers the  primary violence and white people don't  want to hear this the primary terrorism  in this country comes from the  government  but equally he could turn that righteous  fury onto his own supporters in 1985  tutu fearlessly broke up an angry south  african mob and prevented them from  burning a man to death on suspicion of  being an apartheid spy  when the dark days of racial hatred  finally ended and tutu's longtime friend  and former south african president  nelson mandela was released from 27  years behind bars the irrepressible arch  as he is affectionately known could not  hold back his joy hey  freedom is coming freedom is coming and  it was mandela who appointed his friend  tutu to lead south africa's troost and  reconciliation commission  a body tasked with uncovering the truth  about the apartheid government's  brutality at times it moved tutu to  tears  the business of dismantling apartheid  was not all doom and gloom tutu had a  self-deprecating sense of humor one one  lady said to me hey bishop  if they do this when  you come here can you imagine what is  going to happen when nelson comes  quick to crack a joke or break out into  song and dance  the world is a little darker without him  he leaves behind a foundation committed  to human rights  but his real legacy is the wonderful  tale of how a diminutive pastor with a  big laugh  became a global conscience for the world  for cbs morning's debra patter  johannesburg  you know he really seemed to use his  oppression to empathize with others he  was a huge advocate in the lgbtq  community as well but my favorite book  the book of joy is a book about desmond  tutu and the dalai lama and one thing he  says in there is he says we should ask  how are we because we should  collectively all appreciate one another  and each other's achievements that's  right i know at one point in his life he  recognized that he had a powerful voice  and dean decided to use that voice as a  proponent for non-violence you know he  once said um overwhelmed by the extent  of evil he said it was necessary to open  the wound in order to cleanse it  incredible man between him and nelson  mandela they did so much for south  africa and that laugh  yeah  light up a room very memorable
 

